      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 10,
        "titulo": "Wo? and Wohin?: \"in\" and \"into\"",
        "explicacao": "With «in», German separates WHERE you are (Wo?) from WHERE you're going (Wohin?) — the way English separates \"in\" from \"into\", or old English \"where\" from \"whither\". Wo? uses the DATIVE: masculine and neuter «im» (in dem), feminine «in der». Wohin? uses the ACCUSATIVE: masculine «in den», feminine «in die», neuter «ins» (in das). To go to a place there's also «zu» (always dative): zum (zu dem) Bahnhof, zur (zu der) Post; and «nach» for cities and countries: nach Berlin. The full cases come in lessons 11 to 13; for now, memorize the chart.",
        "comparacao": "English changes the preposition: \"I'm in the park\" vs \"I'm going into / to the park\". German keeps the same preposition («in») and changes the article instead: «im Park» (in) vs «in den Park» (into). And \"to\" has three German versions: «in» for buildings you enter, «zu» for people and places you go up to, «nach» for cities and countries.",
        "tabela": [
          {
            "genero": "masculine",
            "wo": "im Park",
            "wohin": "in den Park",
            "zu": "zum Park"
          },
